{
  "dom_id": "step-q79194",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/a41a721eab2eb97e8b7b30ddf877e37a48d2b74eeef82ab0a4b72f3bb4a93603.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(2x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$",
  "worked_solution": "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(2x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=tan\\,(2x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069862/assets/f08846ce1f79cd14c593fbf37360fb8169a26704c956506ca0deedb8025e3b39.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(2x)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,\u27f9\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$",
